# Agent Clients

An Agent Client connects Pontia to a coding agent's native session, control channel, and conversation history. The agent still runs locally with its own credentials, configuration, tools, and filesystem permissions.

Pontia includes these Agent Clients:

| Client | Local requirement | Integration installed by `pontia init` |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [pi](/docs/agent-clients/pi) | `pi` and `tmux` | Pontia's pi plugin |
| [Codex](/docs/codex) | A Codex CLI with app-server daemon support | Per-user Codex daemon startup |
| [Claude Agent](https://github.com/anthod0/pontia/blob/main/docs/claude-agent.md) | Node.js LTS, npm, and Claude Code authentication | The `@pontia/claude-client` package from npm |
| ACP agents | An agent that speaks the Agent Client Protocol over stdio | None; enable the entry after installing the agent |

## Choose Clients during initialization

Run:

```sh
pontia init
```

The initializer checks your selected Clients before applying the configuration. pi is selected by default; Codex and `claude-agent` are optional. Selected Clients are enabled for new Sessions.

<h2 id="enable-an-acp-agent">Enable an ACP agent</h2>

Pontia provides ACP entries for Grok Build (`grok agent stdio`), Kimi CLI (`kimi acp`), and OpenCode (`opencode acp`). Install the agent, then enable its entry in the Dashboard under **Settings → Agent Clients**, or run:

```sh
pontia client enable opencode
```

To add another agent, use **Add custom client** in the same settings page, or define it in `$PONTIA_HOME/config.toml`:

```toml
[acp.agents.company-agent]
name = "Company Agent"
command = "/opt/company-agent/bin/agent"
args = ["--acp"]
```

Instead of `command` and `args`, an entry can set `registry_id` to launch an agent from the ACP registry. The agent must support loading and closing sessions; Pontia reports a missing capability when the Session starts.

Running `pontia init` again preserves the existing External API token and Workspace Browser roots. It can also install or repair a selected Client integration.

## Client capabilities differ

The Dashboard presents the controls supported by the selected Client, such as interruption, model changes, history branching, and queued delivery.

A Pontia Session is durably associated with one client-native conversation identity after binding. Reopening the same native conversation continues the existing Session; a new native conversation belongs to a new Session.

## Client credentials and permissions

Each Client uses its own credentials, sandbox, and local permissions. Sign in to each Client and verify that it can perform the intended work before starting the integration.

See [Troubleshooting](/docs/troubleshooting) when a Client is missing, cannot start, or stops reporting output.
